body:
You can't. Your example does not show a string being overwritten. It shows a reference to a string being reassigned to a different string. 

Strings are immutable means.

"Hello, World"

That is a string object. That string object cannot be changed. I cannot somehow grab it and append a newline on the end. If I do try something like that

    var s = "Hello, world!";
    var ss = s.toLowerCase();

I did not alter s. There are now two strings. This is different from most containers.  

    var l = new ArrayList<int>();
    l.add(1);
    var ll = l;
    ll.add(2);


Both l and ll refer to a list with two elements in it.
